# Running jobs

What a runner does with jobs while your app runs, what happens when nodes
come and go, and how to see and change jobs.

## How a runner takes jobs

A runner has a producer for each of its queues. It takes due jobs from
its queue while it has room, highest priority and earliest first, and runs
each in a process of its own. Taking a job marks it as running on this
node in the same statement that finds it, so two nodes never take the
same job: PostgreSQL skips rows another node has locked, and SQLite has
one writer.

A producer looks for jobs:

- when a job is queued on this node, as soon as the transaction commits
- on PostgreSQL, when any node queues one: inserting a job sends
  `NOTIFY howdy_jobs`, which the server delivers when the transaction
  commits, and each runner listens on a connection of its own
- every second otherwise, which `jobs.check_every` changes

Runners on nodes connected in an Erlang cluster also wake each other
directly. A job queued to run later is found by the poll once it is due.

The listening connection is opened with the settings of the Repo's pool.
A Repo built some other way needs them given:
`jobs.listen_via(runner, pog_config)`. `jobs.without_listening` turns it
off, and the producers poll.

Only jobs this runner has a handler for are taken. During a rolling
deploy, nodes still on the old code leave jobs of a new kind to the nodes
that know it.

## When nodes stop

A node renews its claim on each job it runs every 20 seconds. Stopping
a runner cleanly, as the app's supervisor does on `SIGTERM`, stops it
taking new jobs and gives running ones `jobs.shutdown_grace` (15 seconds by
default) to finish. Those that do not are stopped and handed back without
counting the attempt, for another node to take the next time it looks.

A node that stops without saying so, such as one whose machine is lost,
leaves its jobs claimed. Once a claim has gone a minute without being
renewed, any runner puts the job back as if the attempt had failed, and
it is run again. `jobs.lease(runner, milliseconds)` changes the minute:
shorter notices sooner, at the cost of renewing more often.

This is why a job is done at least once. Write handlers so that doing a
job a second time is harmless.

## Pausing

`jobs.pause(repo, queue)` stops every node taking jobs from a queue until
`jobs.resume(repo, queue)`. Jobs keep being queued, and running ones
finish. Use it to hold back a queue whose jobs call a service that is
down, rather than spending their attempts.

## Watching jobs

### In the admin

Register the runner with the [admin area](/docs/admin/overview):

```gleam
admin.new() |> admin.jobs(runner)
```

The Jobs page follows the database as it changes:

- each queue, how many of its jobs this node runs at once, how many are in
  each state, and a button to pause or resume it
- the runner's schedules, when each last queued a job and when it is next
  due
- the jobs, newest first, filtered by state, queue and job, with a button
  to run a waiting job now, retry a failed one, or cancel one

Each job has a page of its own, with its arguments and every failure, to
retry, cancel or delete it. The admin is for development; the runner need
not be running in the same process for its jobs to show.

### On a server

`howdy/jobs/cli` is a command line for the same, in a task module of your
app:

```gleam
// src/tasks/jobs.gleam
import howdy/jobs/cli

pub fn main() -> Nil {
  cli.main(fn() { my_app.open_database() })
}
```

```sh
gleam run -m tasks/jobs stats
gleam run -m tasks/jobs list --state failed --job welcome_email
gleam run -m tasks/jobs show 1234
gleam run -m tasks/jobs retry --failed --queue mail
gleam run -m tasks/jobs pause mail
```

In a release built with `gleam export erlang-shipment`, run it from the
release directory with the arguments after `-extra`:

```sh
erl -pa */ebin -noshell -eval 'tasks@jobs:main()' -extra stats
```

`help` lists every command. The same changes are functions on the Repo,
for a console or your own pages: `jobs.list`, `jobs.get`, `jobs.counts`,
`jobs.retry`, `jobs.cancel`, `jobs.delete`, `jobs.pause`, `jobs.resume`
and `jobs.prune`.

### In traces

With [telemetry](/docs/telemetry/overview) running, queueing a job inside
a traced request is an `enqueue <job>` span, and each attempt is a
`job <job>` span of its own trace, linked to the request that queued it.
The span carries the job's id, queue and attempt, and an attempt that did
not succeed marks it failed with the reason. Queries the handler makes
are inside it, as they are in a request.

## States

- `Available`: due, waiting for a free slot
- `Scheduled`: waiting until later, including a job waiting to be tried
  again
- `Executing`: running on a node
- `Completed`: done
- `Cancelled`: cancelled by its handler or by hand
- `Failed`: out of attempts, or given up

Jobs are kept in the `howdy_jobs` table. Times are unix milliseconds, and
arguments and failures are JSON. Finished jobs are deleted after a week,
or `jobs.keep_finished_for`.
